A small `AppConfig` record implements `Serializable`, so it can be written to disk and read back intact on the next startup.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

record AppConfig(String dbUrl, int port, boolean enableCache) implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;
}

public class SerializationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        var config = new AppConfig("jdbc:postgresql://localhost/app", 8080, true);

        // Serialize to file
        try (var out = new ObjectOutputStream(Files.newOutputStream(Path.of("config.ser")))) {
            out.writeObject(config); // writes the object as bytes
        }

        // Deserialize from file
        try (var in = new ObjectInputStream(Files.newInputStream(Path.of("config.ser")))) {
            var loadedConfig = (AppConfig) in.readObject(); // reconstructs the object
            System.out.println("Loaded: " + loadedConfig);
        }
    }
}
```

Notice the symmetry: `writeObject()` and `readObject()` are mirror images, and the explicit `serialVersionUID` keeps old `.ser` files readable after harmless code changes.

Here a `Session` stores a username and a password, but the password is marked `transient`, so it never reaches the byte stream.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class TransientDemo {
    static class Session implements Serializable {
        private String user;
        private transient String password; // NOT serialized
        Session(String u, String p) { user = u; password = p; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var baos = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        try (var out = new ObjectOutputStream(baos)) {
            out.writeObject(new Session("amy", "secret"));
        }
        try (var in = new ObjectInputStream(
                new ByteArrayInputStream(baos.toByteArray()))) {
            Session s = (Session) in.readObject();
            System.out.println(s.user);     // amy
            System.out.println(s.password); // null -> transient was skipped
        }
    }
}
```

The restored `password` is `null`, which is exactly why a real app forces the user to authenticate again after a deserialize.

Both versions of this class keep the same `serialVersionUID`, so a file written by `UserSession` can still be read after the field set grows.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

record UserSession(String userId, long loginTime) implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L; // explicit version control
}

// Later version with a new field but the SAME serialVersionUID for compatibility
record UserSessionV2(String userId, long loginTime, String ipAddress) implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L; // same id allows reading old data
}

public class VersioningDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var session = new UserSession("user123", System.currentTimeMillis());
        try (var out = new ObjectOutputStream(Files.newOutputStream(Path.of("session.ser")))) {
            out.writeObject(session);
        }
        // Keep the id the same -> old data deserializes.
        // Change the id -> Java throws InvalidClassException.
    }
}
```

The rule of thumb falls out of this: keep the id steady for compatible changes, and only bump it when the structure breaks in a way old data can't survive.

For most data crossing a service boundary, a few lines of `Jackson` replace Java serialization with a portable, readable format.

```java
// Java's built-in serialization is binary, Java-only, and fragile across versions.
// JSON via Jackson is cross-language and human-readable:
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

public class SerializationAlternatives {
    record User(String name, int age) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        String json = mapper.writeValueAsString(new User("Amy", 30));
        System.out.println(json);              // {"name":"Amy","age":30}
        User back = mapper.readValue(json, User.class);
        System.out.println(back);              // User[name=Amy, age=30]
    }
}
```

For compact, schema-driven binary between services, swap `Jackson` for Protocol Buffers or Avro and the same idea applies.

This `readObject()` runs a command straight from the deserialized data — a deliberately dangerous shape that shows why attacker-controlled bytes are lethal.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

record AdminCommand(String command) implements Serializable {
    private void readObject(ObjectInputStream in) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        in.defaultReadObject();
        // DANGER: executing a command from deserialized data
        Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command); // attacker controls this!
    }
}

public class VulnerableApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var untrustedBytes = Files.readAllBytes(Path.of("attacker.ser"));
        try (var in = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(untrustedBytes))) {
            var obj = in.readObject(); // executes the malicious command!
        }
        // SAFE alternative: parse JSON into a known type and validate it.
        // var config = new ObjectMapper().readValue(Files.readString(Path.of("config.json")), ConfigData.class);
    }
}
```

The key takeaway is that no method of yours was called on purpose — simply reading the stream was enough to run code.
